// Lesson 6.4 — Double Jump: Boots or Potion
//
// Scaffolding: OUTLINE ONLY (level 3). The student built the flick in 6.1, the
// ground check AND the jump budget in 6.2, and the mercy timers in 6.3, and has
// built a pickup twice (2.1 coin, 2.3 healing potion) — so a double jump is one
// number changing plus a pickup. This lesson therefore names the parts, points
// at where to read each one, lays the API out in a table and stops: no
// walkthrough, no lines handed over. A level-3 lesson must not render a
// walkthrough, and verify-site refuses one that does (the `level >= 3 && wt`
// rule in scripts/verify-site.mjs).
//
// THE CHOICE (Aram's direction): the second jump arrives as a pickup, and the
// student picks which pickup — boots, a PERMANENT raise, or a potion, a
// TEMPORARY window. Both are real games and both are buildable from the same
// budget; what differs is the lifetime of the fact. That is why the lesson is
// level 3: the mechanism was taught in 6.2, and what is left is a decision the
// student has to be able to defend.
//
// WHY THE BUDGET IS NOT RE-TAUGHT HERE: 6.2 now builds `jumpsRemaining` /
// `maxJumps` and the walk-off charge, so the counter and the refill already
// exist in the student's file. 6.4 changes exactly one of them — the maximum —
// and the walk-off rule comes along for free: a player carrying boots who walks
// off a ledge is charged one jump, keeps one. That sentence is in the lead and
// in the checklist on purpose; it is the payoff of 6.2's `-= 1`.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read out of AP_CS_P_Starter_Project, Unity 6000.6.0f1):
//   - ProjectSettings/TagManager.asset declares exactly six tags: Coin, Enemy,
//     Axe, Spikes, Ground, GroundCheck. There is NO `Boots` tag and no jump-potion
//     tag, so the student creates whichever one their choice needs. (The `Player`
//     tag on the Player prefab is a Unity built-in and does not appear in that list.)
//   - There is no boots art anywhere in the project (no file matching *boot*), and
//     the art folder is thin. Greyboxing is the honest instruction, and 2.3
//     already taught it for the potion.
//   - No prefab in the project carries a Rigidbody2D, and exactly ONE carries a
//     Collider2D: Switch.prefab, whose BoxCollider2D is a trigger (m_IsTrigger: 1),
//     with no body. The coin collider is added by the student in 2.1 and the
//     Player's Dynamic body with it, so the trigger pair the pickup needs is
//     already in place.
//   - Unity 6 names: `Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity` (not `velocity`), and
//     `Object.FindFirstObjectByType<T>()` replaces the deprecated
//     `FindObjectOfType<T>()`. The legacy `Input` class is correct.
//   - The build's jump is `rb.AddForceY(jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse)`, not
//     `AddForce(Vector2.up * ...)`. 4.5 established `AddForceY()` and 6.1 used it;
//     an earlier version of this table still had the old call.
//
// STRUCTURE (unchanged): two halves. "In the Editor" is the numbered, pane-named
// half — the tag, the greyboxed prefab, the collider decision, the placement, the
// soft gate. "In the Code" is the API table and the three architectural
// specifications, which is where the student writes the branch.
//
// PROTECTED BLOCKS — ids are POSITIONAL (see src/lib/formify.ts), so nothing here
// is reordered, renumbered or re-counted. The socratic survives in its original
// document position with its original three items; the decompose spec keeps its
// id and its two turns. No nested <div> inside any protected block.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The recurring two-beat block: split the problem, look each part up, connect
// it to something already built. Level 3, so the renderer withholds what each
// part DOES and asks the student to say it — the parts and their doc pointers
// are the level-3 bargain. Every doc pointer is a lesson the student has
// finished (2.1, 2.3, 2.4, 3.3, 6.2, 6.3) and nothing later than today.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'Tap the key in mid-air and jump a second time — but only after you have picked something up. ' +
    'The budget you wrote in 6.2 already counts jumps and already knows how many you are allowed; ' +
    'what it has never had is a reason for that number to be anything but one. So this lesson is ' +
    'not a new mechanic. It is one field changing — and a decision about whether it ever changes ' +
    'back.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Raise the ceiling, not the count',
      does: '`maxJumps` goes from 1 to 2. Nothing else in 6.2\'s budget moves: the refill still refills to the maximum, and the walk-off charge still costs exactly one, so a player carrying the pickup who steps off a ledge keeps the air jump and loses the ground one.',
      doc: '6.2 — Ground Checks: No Infinite Jumps: the two fields in the class body, the refill, and the charge for walking off',
      href: '6.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Decide whether the fact is permanent or temporary',
      does: 'Boots raise the maximum and never lower it again. A potion raises it for a while and then puts it back. Both are legal, both are shipped games, and neither is the "easy" one — but only one of them needs a clock, and only one of them has to survive a scene load.',
      doc: '2.3 — Healing Pickups & the Max-Health Cap, for the pickup that moves a ceiling rather than filling a number',
      href: '2.3.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Build the pickup that does it',
      does: 'The potion pattern from 2.3 with a different payload: a tag of your own, a trigger collider, one more branch in the callback you already have, and a `Destroy()` so the thing does not come back every frame.',
      doc: '2.1 — Coins: Triggers, Tags & Your First Stat (tag, trigger, callback, destroy) and 2.3 for the second pickup',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
    {
      // The temporary path only. This is the part with no precedent in a
      // pickup — the cooldown is the only timer the student has written.
      name: 'Give the temporary version a clock',
      does: 'A `float` that counts down with `Time.deltaTime`, and a duration in the Inspector beside it. You have written exactly this shape once, in 3.3, and it was a cooldown then: a number that goes down while you wait, and a comparison that asks whether it has reached zero.',
      doc: '3.3 — Fire Rate: Cooldowns & Time: the countdown, and `Time.deltaTime` as the unit of "one second of play"',
      href: '3.3.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Decide what happens the frame it runs out',
      does: 'A player can finish a potion mid-air with a jump still unspent. The count has to come back down to the new maximum, in one place, without an error message — or the player keeps a jump they no longer own. Write the rule down before you write the line.',
      doc: 'the refill and the charge in 6.2, which are the only two other places the budget is written',
      href: '6.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Put the pickup where the level can ask for it',
      does: 'An instance standing somewhere a tester has to walk, and a coin moved onto a shelf one jump cannot reach. Nothing is a gate until there is a visible thing you cannot get to.',
      doc: 'the prefab-versus-instance rule and the placement work in 2.1, and the playtest habits from 2.4',
      href: '2.4.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Say what happens to it when you die',
      does: 'The Player is rebuilt from the prefab on a scene reload, so a field on the Player does not survive one. Where the boots — or the potion\'s remaining seconds — live decides whether your pickups are a one-run thing or part of the run you are on.',
      doc: '2.7 — Refactor Day: Moving Stats into the StatManager, and 5.3 for the object that survives a scene load',
      href: '5.3.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Six of those seven parts are things you have already built, and the seventh is a decision. What ' +
    'is genuinely new is small enough to miss: **a capability has a lifetime, and you get to choose ' +
    'it.** Permanent and temporary are not two ways of writing the same feature — they change what ' +
    'your level can ask for. The boots rewrite every gap in the cave, forever. The potion rewrites ' +
    'the gaps you can reach in the next few seconds, and then the level snaps back. The code is one ' +
    'field either way; the choice is the work.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: does the part name something you could point at — a field, a ' +
    'line, a branch, an object in the Hierarchy? "Make a double jump" points at nothing. "Where the ' +
    'counter goes back down when the clock runs out" points at one branch, and that is a part.',
  turns: [
    'Boots raise a ceiling and never lower it; a potion opens a window and then closes it. Neither one is written in fewer lines than the other — say what a player learns from each, and which lesson your cave is trying to teach.',
    'There is exactly one moment in the whole game where the count should go back up, and 6.2 already put it there. Name it — then say what the temporary version has to add that the permanent version never does.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>6.4 — Double Jump: Boots or Potion</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The potion from 2.3 raised a number that already had a ceiling. This pickup changes something
    else entirely: it changes what your <em>level design is allowed to ask for</em>. And it comes in
    two flavours, which are genuinely different games. <strong>Boots</strong> raise your jump
    allowance for the rest of the run — every gap in the cave is yours to widen. A
    <strong>potion</strong> raises it for a few seconds and then takes it back — the cave stays the
    same size, and you get to cross the part of it you could not. Pick one, build it, and be ready
    to say why the other one is worse for <em>your</em> game.
</p>

<p>
    Either way, the machinery is already in your file. <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> left you with a
    budget — <code>jumpsRemaining</code>, <code>maxJumps</code>, a refill on the ground, and a
    charge for walking off a ledge. Today changes <em>one</em> of those fields. And the walk-off
    rule pays off for free: a player carrying boots who steps off a ledge is charged one jump,
    keeps the other. That is the difference between a double jump and a jetpack, and you already
    built it.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('in unity how to make a double jump, how to make a temporary power up that wears off, how to count the jumps a player has left', 3)}

${renderDecompose('6.4', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>On paper, in one sentence:</strong> boots or potion, and what the other one
            would have cost you. Write it in your notebook where you can read it back at the end of
            the period — the checkpoint asks you to defend it, and the editor steps below branch on
            it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Edit → Project Settings → Tags and Layers →</strong> add the tag you need:
            <code>Boots</code>, or something like <code>JumpPotion</code>. Read the list while you
            are in there: it ships six tags and every one is a name somebody has to remember. If
            your healing pickup from <a href="2.3.html">2.3</a> already answers to
            <code>Potion</code>, your jump potion needs its <em>own</em> name — one tag cannot mean
            two different things.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → right-click → 2D Object → Sprites → Square</strong>, and rename it.
            Then <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Artwork</code>, pick a sprite you are
            not using, and drop it into <strong>Inspector → Sprite Renderer → Sprite</strong>. There
            is no boots art anywhere in this project — no file matching <em>boot</em>, no prefab —
            so tint it with <strong>Sprite Renderer → Color</strong> until it could not be mistaken
            for a coin or a health potion. That is greyboxing, and it is what
            <a href="2.3.html">2.3</a> did for the healing potion.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Add Component → Box Collider 2D</strong>. Decide whether
            <strong>Is Trigger</strong> is ticked before you leave this step, and be ready to
            defend the answer: a solid pair of boots is something you trip over, and your player
            already carries the Dynamic body a trigger pair needs.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Tag</strong> → the tag you made. Then <strong>Project window →</strong>
            drag the object out of the <strong>Hierarchy</strong> and into
            <code>Assets/Prefabs</code>: it is a prefab now, and the copy still standing in the cave
            is an instance of it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Scene view →</strong> move that instance somewhere a tester has to walk. Then
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs</code> → drag <code>Coin</code>
            into the <strong>Hierarchy</strong>, and <strong>Scene view →</strong> put it on a
            shelf one jump cannot reach.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play twice — once straight at the coin, once after
            collecting the pickup — and time how long a tester needs to work out what it is for. If
            you chose the potion, watch what happens when it runs out in mid-air, and write down
            what the player saw.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Test the whole loop, including death.</strong> Grab the pickup, then die on purpose.
        If your death rule from <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> reloads the scene, the Player is rebuilt
        from the prefab — so the maximum goes back to its Inspector value, and a potion's remaining
        seconds vanish with it. For the boots that is a real design question: is a permanent
        upgrade permanent <em>for the run</em>, or permanent for the save? Write down what you
        observe, then say which of the two has to change — the death rule, or where the fact lives.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        This is the level-3 substitute for a walkthrough: the API is laid out below and the code is
        not. You write the file. Every row is a call or a declaration you have already typed in
        <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a>, <a href="3.3.html">3.3</a> or <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> — read
        the cost column before you pick.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What you get</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>int maxJumps = 1;</code></td>
            <td>The ceiling you already declared in <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a>, and the one field this whole lesson changes. The refill and the walk-off charge both read it, so raising it is a one-line change — and it reaches both of them at once.</td>
            <td>Written once, in the class body. A second assignment somewhere else — in a pickup, in <code>Start()</code>, in a reload — is a second definition of the rule, and one of the two will eventually be wrong.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>[SerializeField] private float doubleJumpDuration = 8f;</code></td>
            <td>The potion's dial: how many seconds of second jump a player buys. Only the temporary path needs it. <code>[SerializeField]</code> because it is a design number, exactly like <code>jumpForce</code>.</td>
            <td>A number in the Inspector is a promise you can tune without recompiling — and a decision you have to make. There is no correct value; there is only the one your cave was built for.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Time.deltaTime</code></td>
            <td>How long the last frame took, in seconds — the unit that turns a countdown into real time. You wrote this in <a href="3.3.html">3.3</a> and the shape is identical: subtract while the number is above zero.</td>
            <td>Decrement by a whole number per frame and the potion lasts as long as your framerate says it does.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AddForceY(jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse)</code></td>
            <td>The flick from <a href="4.5.html">4.5</a> and <a href="6.1.html">6.1</a> — one instant change in momentum, which physics then spends over the following frames. Both jumps call the same method.</td>
            <td>It divides by mass, so retuning mass silently retunes both jumps.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code> and <code>other.CompareTag("Boots")</code></td>
            <td>The pickup callback and the tag check you have had since <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> — one branch more in a method that already handles the coin and the healing potion.</td>
            <td>It fires once per entry, and only if the tag exists in the project — which is why creating it is step two above.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>other.gameObject</code> and <code>Destroy(Object)</code></td>
            <td>The whole object behind the collider you just touched, and the call that removes it at the end of the frame.</td>
            <td>Plain <code>gameObject</code> is <em>you</em>, and getting the two backwards destroys the player. <code>Destroy(Object)</code> is also deferred, so the object is alive for the rest of the frame.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the ceiling.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>What is <code>maxJumps</code> in a build where the player has picked up nothing? Say it out loud, then check that the field in the Inspector agrees with what you said.</li>
            <li>Where is <code>maxJumps</code> written — how many lines, in how many methods? The refill in <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> reads it; does the pickup write it?</li>
            <li>After the pickup, does <code>jumpsRemaining</code> get refilled immediately, or does the player wait until they land? Say which one you chose and what a tester feels.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: permanent or temporary.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>State your choice, then state what the other one would have done to a level you have already built. "It is easier" is not an answer; "my cave has one gap I want the player to cross once and not again" is.</li>
            <li><em>Temporary path:</em> where does the remaining time live, what decrements it, and which comparison decides that the effect is over? Write the sentence before you write the line.</li>
            <li><em>Temporary path:</em> a player finishes the potion in mid-air with a jump unspent. What should happen to <code>jumpsRemaining</code> that frame, and which line does it without an error message?</li>
            <li><em>Permanent path:</em> the maximum is raised forever — forever <em>what</em>? Say which of your death rule, your scene load and your prefab rebuild can undo it, and whether you accept that.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the pickup branch.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which callback fires when the player touches it — the trigger one or the collision one? The answer depends on the collider setting you chose in the editor half, and it changes the <em>parameter type</em> you are handed.</li>
            <li>How do you identify the pickup from inside that callback, and how do you reach the object that should disappear?</li>
            <li>If you chose the potion: what happens if the player finds a second one while the first is still running? Add, replace, or clamp — pick one and say why.</li>
            <li>What do you hand to <code>Debug.Log(string message)</code> so a tester can see the change with no UI in the game?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Deploy in neutral first.</strong> Write the pickup branch with no effect on the
        jump at all — destroy the object, log a line, change nothing. The player should play exactly
        as they did before. Turn the effect on only once that is true: a tag that fires is easier to
        find than a jump that does not.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Choosing the Data</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Where does the jump allowance live — on the player, on the pickup, or on a manager? Your answer should sound like the one you gave in <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a>, and for the same reason.</li>
<li>If you chose the potion, what is the type of "how much longer does this last", and what is its value a second after the player picks it up? If you chose the boots, what is the type of "do I have them", and where is that field after a death?</li>
<li>Your walk-off rule from 6.2 charges one jump when you leave the ground without jumping. With the pickup in the game, is that still the rule you want — or does the second jump make it feel like a punishment? Say what the player experiences, not what the code does.</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Say which field changed in 6.2's budget, and why nothing else had to</li>
        <li>Explain why carrying boots off a ledge still leaves you one jump</li>
        <li>Defend boots over potion, or potion over boots, in terms of a level you built</li>
        <li>If temporary: say where the clock lives, what it counts in, and what happens the frame it runs out mid-air</li>
        <li>If permanent: say what can undo it, and whether you accept that</li>
        <li>Build a pickup end to end — tag, collider, effect — the way you built the healing potion</li>
        <li>Design a soft gate: visible, unreachable, no damage, no text</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

